2. Prior-Art Foundations

2.1 Bayesian Search: Two Distinct Models

The Koopman–Stone model (Koopman 1946; Stone 1975) places a target in exactly one of n boxes. A failed search of box i raises the posterior for all j\ne i. The framework of §3 uses independent validity: rejecting candidate i leaves p_j unchanged for j\ne i. These are different models, and the framework of this paper uses the independent one throughout.

Koopman–Stone posterior (single-target, cited for reference only):

P(\text{target in } i \mid \text{no detection in } i)=\frac{\epsilon_i p_i}{1-(1-\epsilon_i)p_i}.

Independent-candidate posterior (used here):

P(A_i=1\mid O_i=\mathrm{rej})=\frac{\epsilon_i p_i}{1-(1-\epsilon_i)p_i},\qquad P(A_j=1\mid O_i=\mathrm{rej})=p_j,\quad j\ne i.

2.2 Least-Cost Sequential Testing

The ordering rule \alpha_i/c_i descending is the classical solution to the least-cost testing sequence problem, solved analytically by Mitten (1960), Journal of Industrial Engineering 11(1):17. The same combinatorial structure is described in the scheduling literature as a special case of weighted shortest processing time (WSPT), also known as Smith's rule (Smith 1956): sequence jobs by non-increasing density v_j/p_j. The literature on sequential testing with imperfect tests extends this to tests that may report false negatives (Gluss 1959, for fault detection in modular systems, Operations Research 7(4):468–477; Wei et al. 2017, for k-out-of-n systems).

2.3 Pandora's Box, Reservation Values, and Uninspected Options

Weitzman (1979), Econometrica 47(3):641–654, characterized the optimal search rule for sequential inspection of boxes: assign reservation values, inspect in descending order, and stop when the best observed prize weakly exceeds the maximum reservation value among uninspected boxes.

Doval (2018), Journal of Economic Theory 175:127–158, relaxes Weitzman's assumption that the agent may take only boxes already inspected, allowing submission of an uninspected option at the stopping time. She shows that this modification changes both the inspection order and the stopping rule, and that a simple reservation-value index is in general not optimal. This is directly relevant to §7: the n-candidate model permits submitting an unverified candidate, and therefore falls under the Doval-type generalization rather than the classical Weitzman framework.

2.4 Value of Computational Information

Halpern and Pass (2010/2011), "I Don't Want to Think About It Now: Decision Theory With Costly Computation," Proc. KR 2010; arXiv:1106.2657, define the value of computational information (a computational variant of value of information) and the computational value of conversation. This is the correct source for the notion that computation itself has decision-theoretic value.

Halpern and Pucella (2011), "Dealing with logical omniscience: Expressiveness and pragmatics," Artificial Intelligence 175(1):220–235, examine four approaches to logical omniscience, including algorithmic knowledge, where an agent knows \phi if its knowledge algorithm returns "Yes" on a query of \phi. The claim that "logical omniscience makes verified facts worthless" is an inference from this framework, not a statement in Halpern–Pucella.

2.5 Terminal Computation Allocation

Tuisov (2026), "Search as Computation Allocation," arXiv:2607.27871v1 [cs.AI], 30 July 2026 (single author, preprint), formalizes terminal computation-allocation problems: costly computations produce observations, update beliefs about a latent environment, and matter only through terminal decision loss. The paper relates VOC to information: mutual information equals myopic VOC under log loss; under simple regret, VOC is a knowledge-gradient quantity; and information gain gives a one-sided upper bound on myopic VOC for bounded target-dependent losses. The upper bound requires loss depending on \Theta only through the decision-relevant target Z.

2.6 Non-Concavity and Metareasoning

· Blackwell (1953), Annals of Mathematical Statistics 24(2):265–272: comparison of information structures. The asymmetric verifier is an information structure whose value must be evaluated decision-theoretically.
· Radner and Stiglitz (1984), in Bayesian Models in Economic Theory: a small amount of information can have negative marginal net value when its marginal cost is strictly positive.
· Russell and Wefald (1991), Artificial Intelligence 49(1–3):361–395: metareasoning as a sequential decision problem; the value of a computation derives from its effect on the actions chosen.
· Hay et al. (2012), Proc. UAI: selecting computations as a sequential decision problem with utility-theoretic evaluation.

