3. The Terminal Model

3.1 Terminal Loss

Definition 3.1 (Terminal loss). A meta-level action a\in\{1,\dots,n\}\cup\{\bot\} selects a candidate proof object or the null submission. The terminal loss is

L(a,Z)=
\begin{cases}
P_{\mathrm{bad}}\cdot\mathbf{1}\{Z(a)=0\}, & a\in\{1,\dots,n\},\\[4pt]
F, & a=\bot,
\end{cases}

where P_{\mathrm{bad}}>0 is the penalty for submitting an invalid proof, F\ge 0 is the failure cost of submitting nothing, and Z(a)\in\{0,1\} is the validity of candidate a. The null action incurs F regardless of Z.

3.2 The Asymmetric Verification Channel

Definition 3.2 (Sound-incomplete verifier). Let A\in\{0,1\} denote ground-truth validity of a candidate proof object and O\in\{\mathrm{acc},\mathrm{rej}\} the verifier output. The verifier is sound but incomplete:

P(O=\mathrm{acc}\mid A=0)=0,\qquad P(O=\mathrm{rej}\mid A=1)=\epsilon>0.

Remark 3.1 (Source of \epsilon). In Lean and similar kernels, proof checking is sound and complete for the formal system: a valid proof term is always accepted given sufficient resources. The parameter \epsilon>0 models resource limits — timeouts, heartbeat limits, memory exhaustion, or tactic failure on a solvable goal — not kernel unsoundness.

Lemma 3.1 (Acceptance probability). With p_A=P(A=1):

P(O=\mathrm{acc})=(1-\epsilon)\,p_A.

Proof. Law of total probability, soundness, incompleteness. \blacksquare

Lemma 3.2 (Rejection posterior). With \alpha_i=p_i(1-\epsilon_i):

P(A_i=1\mid O_i=\mathrm{rej})=\frac{\epsilon_i p_i}{1-\alpha_i}.

Proof. Bayes' theorem with denominator 1-P(O_i=\mathrm{acc})=1-\alpha_i. \blacksquare

Corollary 3.1 (Certification asymmetry). If 0<p_A<1 and 0<\epsilon<1, then P(A=1\mid O=\mathrm{acc})=1 and 0<P(A=1\mid O=\mathrm{rej})<1. \blacksquare

3.3 The n-Candidate Model

Definition 3.3 (n-candidate verification). There are n candidates with independent validity A_i\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(p_i). A verification of candidate i costs c_i and returns O_i\in\{\mathrm{acc},\mathrm{rej}\} with

P(O_i=\mathrm{acc}\mid A_i=1)=1-\epsilon_i,\qquad P(O_i=\mathrm{acc}\mid A_i=0)=0.

At any point, the agent may:

1. Verify an unverified candidate i at cost c_i;
2. Submit a candidate j (verified-accepted, verified-rejected, or unverified);
3. Submit the null action \bot.

If all candidates are rejected, the agent submits the rejected candidate with the highest posterior validity rather than being forced into \bot.

Definition 3.4 (Terminal best-guess loss). After all candidates reject, the agent submits

j^\star=\operatorname*{arg\,max}_i \frac{\epsilon_i p_i}{1-\alpha_i},

and the terminal loss is

L_{\mathrm{term}}=P_{\mathrm{bad}}\cdot\left(1-\max_i \frac{\epsilon_i p_i}{1-\alpha_i}\right).

3.4 n=1 Sanity Check

For n=1, verification cannot change the decision. The expected loss without verification is P_{\mathrm{bad}}(1-p_1). With verification, the outcomes are:

· Accept (probability \alpha_1): loss 0.
· Reject (probability 1-\alpha_1): submit the rejected candidate, expected loss P_{\mathrm{bad}}\left(1-P(A_1=1\mid O_1=\mathrm{rej})\right).

Expected terminal loss:

\mathbb{E}[L_{\mathrm{term}}]=\alpha_1\cdot 0+(1-\alpha_1)\cdot P_{\mathrm{bad}}\left(1-\frac{\epsilon_1 p_1}{1-\alpha_1}\right)
=P_{\mathrm{bad}}\left[(1-\alpha_1)-\epsilon_1 p_1\right]
=P_{\mathrm{bad}}(1-p_1),

using 1-\alpha_1-\epsilon_1 p_1=1-p_1(1-\epsilon_1)-\epsilon_1 p_1=1-p_1.

Gross DV is zero, as Theorem 4.1 will require. Net DV is -c_1.

3.5 n=2 Illustration (Restricted Fixed-Order Policy)

Let p_1=0.5, p_2=0.3, \epsilon_1=\epsilon_2=0.1, c_1=c_2=0.01, P_{\mathrm{bad}}=1, F=1. Then \alpha_1=0.45, \alpha_2=0.27. Rejection posteriors:

P(A_1=1\mid O_1=\mathrm{rej})\approx0.0909,\qquad P(A_2=1\mid O_2=\mathrm{rej})\approx0.0411.

Terminal best-guess after both reject: j^\star=1, L_{\mathrm{term}}=0.9091.

Blind cost (submit candidate 1 without verification): C_{\mathrm{blind}}=0.5.

Fixed-order cost (verify 1 then 2, committed to verifying both unless accepted):

C_{\mathrm{full}}(1,2)=c_1+(1-\alpha_1)c_2+(1-\alpha_1)(1-\alpha_2)L_{\mathrm{term}}
=0.01+0.0055+0.365=0.3805.

Gross DV (reduction in expected terminal loss, ignoring verification costs): 0.5-0.365=0.135. Net DV: 0.5-0.3805=0.1195. Break-even cost (uniform c): 0.135/1.55\approx0.0871.

Remark 3.2. The value 0.3805 is the cost of a restricted committed policy, not the optimal cost of the general adaptive problem. The general problem may do better by submitting candidate 2 unverified after rejecting candidate 1.

