4. Myopic VOC_1 and Dynamic DV

4.1 Definitions

Definition 4.1 (Myopic VOC_1). For a single observation o from state s:

VOC_1(o;s)=R(s)-R(s,o),

where R(s)=\min_a \mathbb{E}[C(a,\Theta)\mid s] and R(s,o)=\min_a \mathbb{E}[C(a,\Theta)\mid s,o]. The ex-ante myopic value is VOC_1(s)=\mathbb{E}_O[VOC_1(O;s)].

Definition 4.2 (Dynamic DV). The Bellman decision value from state s for a query o is

DV(o;s)=C^\star(s)-\mathbb{E}_{O\mid s}[C^\star(s\mid O)],

where C^\star is the optimal cost-to-go. Dynamic DV accounts for future verification options; VOC_1 does not.

4.2 Zero-Value Characterization

Theorem 4.1 (Exact zero-value characterization for VOC_1). Let \mathcal{A} be finite. Then VOC_1(s)=0 iff there exists a^\star\in\arg\min_a\mathbb{E}[C(a,\Theta)\mid s] such that a^\star\in\arg\min_a\mathbb{E}[C(a,\Theta)\mid s,o] for P-a.e. o.

Proof. Law of total expectation and nonnegativity of the integrand. \blacksquare

Remark 4.1. Theorem 4.1 holds for VOC_1, not for dynamic DV. The exact zero characterization for dynamic DV requires a stronger condition: no future decision tree is changed by the observation. This is open.

4.3 Gap Decomposition

Theorem 4.2 (Gap decomposition for VOC_1). Define

\mathrm{gap}(o)=\mathbb{E}[C(a^\star,\Theta)\mid s,o]-\mathbb{E}[C(a^\star(o),\Theta)\mid s,o]\ge0,

where a^\star\in\arg\min_a\mathbb{E}[C(a,\Theta)\mid s] and a^\star(o)\in\arg\min_a\mathbb{E}[C(a,\Theta)\mid s,o]. Then

\boxed{VOC_1(s)=\mathbb{E}_O[\mathrm{gap}(O)]=\sum_o P(o\mid s)\,\mathrm{gap}(o)}

for a finite observation space, or \int \mathrm{gap}(o)\,dP(o\mid s) for a continuous one. With p_{\mathrm{change}}=P(a^\star\notin\arg\min_a\mathbb{E}[C(a,\Theta)\mid s,o]) and

g_{\min}=\operatorname*{ess\,inf}_{o:\,a^\star\notin\arg\min_a\mathbb{E}[C(a,\Theta)\mid s,o]} \mathrm{gap}(o),\qquad
g_{\max}=\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_o \mathrm{gap}(o),

we have

p_{\mathrm{change}}\cdot g_{\min}\le VOC_1(s)\le p_{\mathrm{change}}\cdot g_{\max}.

Proof. Immediate from Definition 4.1 and nonnegativity of \mathrm{gap}. \blacksquare

Remark 4.2. The lower bound is non-vacuous only if g_{\min}>0. For continuous observation spaces, the essential infimum is used.

