5. Additivity and Complementarity

5.1 Additivity Requires Additive Terminal Loss

Definition 5.1 (Independent AND-components). Subgoals i,j are independent if:

1. Resolving i does not change the cost or feasibility of j;
2. Costs are additive: C(s_i\wedge s_j,a_i,a_j)=C(s_i,a_i)+C(s_j,a_j);
3. Transitions factorize: T(s_i'\mid s_i,a_i)T(s_j'\mid s_j,a_j);
4. No shared variables, hypotheses, or metavariables;
5. No shared resources (verifier, cache, memory, budget);
6. Observations factorize: P(o_i,o_j\mid s_i,s_j)=P(o_i\mid s_i)P(o_j\mid s_j).

Counterexample to simple additivity. Let s=A\wedge B, each subgoal costs 1, succeeds with probability 1/2, failure incurs penalty P=10. Then C^\star(A)=6, C^\star(B)=6, but C^\star(A\wedge B)=9\ne12, because failure of A terminates the proof and avoids the cost of attempting B.

Theorem 5.1 (Additivity under additive terminal loss). Under Definition 5.1 plus the condition

L_{\mathrm{terminal}}(s_1\wedge\cdots\wedge s_r)=\sum_{k=1}^r L_k(s_k),

we have

C^\star(s_1\wedge\cdots\wedge s_r)=\sum_{k=1}^r C^\star(s_k).

Proof. The Bellman equation decomposes because the terminal loss and the transitions factorize. \blacksquare

Remark 5.1. Additivity fails under discounting, a shared budget or horizon, or cost functions coupling subgoals.

5.2 Complementarity Index

Definition 5.2 (Complementarity indices). For two observation channels O_1,O_2 at a common initial state s:

\kappa_{\mathrm{VOC}}=VOC_1(O_1,O_2;s)-VOC_1(O_1;s)-VOC_1(O_2;s),

\kappa_{\mathrm{net}}=DV_{\mathrm{net}}(O_1,O_2;s)-DV_{\mathrm{net}}(O_1;s)-DV_{\mathrm{net}}(O_2;s),

where DV_{\mathrm{net}} denotes dynamic DV net of verification costs. The two indices differ in general; keeping them distinct prevents conflating gross information value with net decision value.

Theorem 5.2 (Sign of \kappa). \kappa_{\mathrm{VOC}} can be positive, zero, or negative.

Positive example (XOR). Let X,Y\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(1/2) independent, a^\star=X\oplus Y, cost 0 for correct and 1 otherwise. Observations o_1=X, o_2=Y. Then VOC_1(o_1)=VOC_1(o_2)=0, VOC_1(o_1,o_2)=1/2, so \kappa_{\mathrm{VOC}}=1/2>0.

Negative example (redundant observations). Let X\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(1/2), a^\star=X, cost 0 for correct and 1 otherwise. Observations o_1=o_2=X. Then VOC_1(o_1)=VOC_1(o_1,o_2)=1/2, so

\kappa_{\mathrm{VOC}}=1/2-1/2-1/2=-1/2<0.

Zero example. Independent observations on disjoint components of an additive cost yield \kappa_{\mathrm{VOC}}=0 by Theorem 5.1. \blacksquare

