6. Fixed-Order Verification as a Restricted Policy Class

6.1 The Restricted Problem

Definition 6.1 (Fixed-order committed policy). A fixed-order committed policy is a pair (\sigma,S) with S\subseteq\{1,\dots,n\} and \sigma an ordering of S. Under this policy, the agent verifies \sigma(1),\sigma(2),\dots in order, stopping at the first acceptance; if all verifications reject, the agent submits the best-guess candidate from S.

The restricted class is denoted \Pi_{\mathrm{fix}}.

Definition 6.2 (Fixed-order cost). For (\sigma,S)\in\Pi_{\mathrm{fix}}:

\boxed{
C_{\mathrm{full}}(\sigma,S)=\sum_{k=1}^{|S|}\left[\prod_{j<k}(1-\alpha_{\sigma(j)})\right]c_{\sigma(k)}+\left[\prod_{j=1}^{|S|}(1-\alpha_{\sigma(j)})\right]L_{\mathrm{term}}(S).
}

Remark 6.1. The terminal term L_{\mathrm{term}}(S) depends only on the set S, not on the order, because the rejection posterior depends on the candidate alone (independent-validity model) and the product \prod_{j\in S}(1-\alpha_j) is order-independent.

6.2 Classical Ratio Rule

Theorem 6.1 (Optimal ordering for fixed S). Fix S\subseteq\{1,\dots,n\}. The order minimizing C_{\mathrm{full}}(\cdot,S) over orderings of S is by decreasing \alpha_i/c_i=p_i(1-\epsilon_i)/c_i.

Proof (adjacent interchange). Fix positions k,k+1 with candidates i,j, and let \beta=\prod_{m<k}(1-\alpha_{\sigma(m)}). The contribution of the two positions is

\beta[c_i+(1-\alpha_i)c_j]\quad\text{for order }(i,j),

\beta[c_j+(1-\alpha_j)c_i]\quad\text{for order }(j,i).

The terminal term is identical because (1-\alpha_i)(1-\alpha_j)=(1-\alpha_j)(1-\alpha_i). Therefore (i,j) is at least as good as (j,i) iff

c_i+(1-\alpha_i)c_j\le c_j+(1-\alpha_j)c_i
\iff \alpha_j c_i\le\alpha_i c_j
\iff \frac{\alpha_i}{c_i}\ge\frac{\alpha_j}{c_j}.

Any order with an adjacent inversion is strictly improvable by swapping. \blacksquare

Remark 6.2 (Classical origin). This is the least-cost sequential-testing ratio rule (Mitten 1960), also a special case of WSPT / Smith's rule. It is a restricted result: it applies to committed fixed-order policies with a fixed set, not to the general adaptive problem of §7.

