7. General Adaptive Verification and Submission

7.1 State, History, and Action Space

Definition 7.1 (History). A history is

H_t=(i_1,o_1,\tau_1,\dots,i_t,o_t,\tau_t),

where i_k is the candidate verified at step k, o_k\in\{\mathrm{acc},\mathrm{rej}\} is the observation, and \tau_k is the resource budget. Let \mathcal{H} be the set of all histories.

Definition 7.2 (Action space). At history H_t, the agent chooses

a\in\{\mathrm{verify}(i,\tau): i\notin V(H_t),\,\tau>0\}\cup\{\mathrm{submit}(j): j\in\{1,\dots,n\}\}\cup\{\bot\},

where V(H_t) is the set of candidates already verified.

Definition 7.3 (General adaptive value). The optimal value satisfies

\boxed{
V(H)=\min\left\{
V_{\mathrm{submit}}(H),\ V_{\bot}(H),\ \min_{i\notin V(H),\,\tau>0}\left[c_i(\tau)+\mathbb{E}_{O_i\mid H,\tau}\left[V(H,i,O_i,\tau)\right]\right]
\right\},
}

with

V_{\mathrm{submit}}(H)=\min_{j\in\{1,\dots,n\}} P_{\mathrm{bad}}\cdot P(A_j=0\mid H),
\qquad V_{\bot}(H)=F.

Remark 7.1. The general problem is a sequential adaptive decision problem: both the continuation decision (stop vs. continue) and the next verified candidate i_{t+1} may depend on H_t. Because the model allows submission of unverified candidates, it is an instance of the Doval (2018) generalization of Pandora's box rather than the classical Weitzman (1979) framework.

7.2 Specializations

Theorem 7.1 (Fixed-order as a restriction). The class \Pi_{\mathrm{fix}} is a strict subset of the adaptive policies satisfying Definition 7.2. For (\sigma,S)\in\Pi_{\mathrm{fix}}, the value is C_{\mathrm{full}}(\sigma,S) from Definition 6.2.

Proof. Fixed-order committed policies are adaptive policies whose continuation rule is "continue while k<|S| and no acceptance" and whose next candidate is determined by \sigma. \blacksquare

Corollary 7.1 (Hierarchy).

\boxed{
\text{classical sequencing}\ \subset\ \text{fixed-order committed}\ \subset\ \text{adaptive verification-and-submission}.
}

Remark 7.2 (Non-optimality of the restricted class). The optimal adaptive policy may strictly dominate every fixed-order committed policy, because it can submit an unverified candidate after a rejection, or stop before exhausting a fixed set S.

7.3 Stopping and Selection

Conjecture 7.1 (Reservation-value stopping with Doval correction). Under regularity conditions on the distributions of T_i^{(1)} and T_i^{(0)}, the optimal adaptive policy admits a state-dependent index I_i(H,\tau) such that

\pi^\star(H)=\operatorname*{arg\,max}_{i,\tau} I_i(H,\tau)

when the decision is to verify, with stopping determined by the comparison of I_i(H,\tau) to V_{\mathrm{submit}}(H) and V_\bot(H). The index is not in general equal to \alpha_i/c_i, because the option to submit an unverified candidate modifies the continuation value. Proof is open.

Remark 7.3. This conjecture is deliberately weaker than v0.7's Conjecture 9.1: it is state-dependent, not a fixed per-candidate index, and it is an existence claim under regularity, not a closed-form rule.

