8. Resource-Dependent Verification: A Lean-Specific Modeling Hypothesis

8.1 The Hypothesis

Hypothesis 8.1 (Resource-dependent verification error). In a proof assistant such as Lean, verification of candidate i consumes a resource budget \tau (heartbeats). The verification error is

\epsilon_i(\tau)=1-F_i^{(1)}(\tau),

where F_i^{(1)} is the CDF of the resource requirement T_i^{(1)} of candidate i conditional on validity. Under this hypothesis, retries at the same budget \tau are perfectly correlated: a candidate that times out at \tau will time out again at \tau.

Remark 8.1. Hypothesis 8.1 is a modeling hypothesis, not a proven novelty. To become a substantive contribution, it must be shown that (i) previous verification models assume \epsilon fixed, (ii) the resource-dependent model is necessary to represent Lean heartbeats, and (iii) this dependence modifies a non-trivial decision-theoretic property.

8.2 Validity-Conditioned Cost

Definition 8.1 (Validity-conditioned verification cost). With T_i^{(1)} the resource requirement given validity and T_i^{(0)} given invalidity:

\boxed{
c_i(\tau)=\lambda_V\left[p_i\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\min(T_i^{(1)},\tau)\right]+(1-p_i)\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\min(T_i^{(0)},\tau)\right]\right].
}

Remark 8.2. The distinction matters: a valid proof may require large computation, while an invalid candidate may be rejected quickly. Collapsing to a single distribution T_i would misstate both \epsilon_i(\tau) and c_i(\tau).

8.3 Acceptance Probability Under Budget

Lemma 8.1. Under Hypothesis 8.1:

\alpha_i(\tau)=p_i\left(1-\epsilon_i(\tau)\right)=p_i\,F_i^{(1)}(\tau).

Lemma 8.2 (Escalation). After a rejection at budget \tau_1, escalating to \tau_2>\tau_1 succeeds given validity with probability

\frac{F_i^{(1)}(\tau_2)-F_i^{(1)}(\tau_1)}{1-F_i^{(1)}(\tau_1)}.

8.4 The Ratio Under Resource Dependence

Definition 8.2 (Resource-dependent ratio). For a fixed budget \tau:

\rho_i(\tau)=\frac{\alpha_i(\tau)}{c_i(\tau)}=\frac{p_i\,F_i^{(1)}(\tau)}{\lambda_V\left[p_i\,\mathbb{E}[\min(T_i^{(1)},\tau)]+(1-p_i)\,\mathbb{E}[\min(T_i^{(0)},\tau)]\right]}.

Remark 8.3. The ratio \rho_i(\tau) is a function of \tau, and the budget enters the action space of the adaptive problem. This is the substantive structural difference from classical search theory, where \epsilon is fixed.

