9. Two-Sided Bounds

9.1 Lower Bound

Theorem 9.1 (Exact gap decomposition for VOC_1). For myopic VOC_1:

VOC_1(s)=\mathbb{E}_O[\mathrm{gap}(O)]\ge p_{\mathrm{change}}\cdot g_{\min}.

9.2 Upper Bound with Pinsker

Theorem 9.2 (Tuisov upper bound for myopic VOC_1). Assume:

1. L(a,Z) is bounded by L_{\max};
2. L depends on \Theta only through Z=\zeta(\Theta);
3. IG(s)=I(Z;O\mid s) in nats.

Then

\boxed{VOC_1(s)\le L_{\max}\sqrt{\frac{IG(s)}{2}}.}

Proof (Pinsker, sup-norm TV). Let P be the prior on Z and Q_o the posterior given O=o. With \|P-Q_o\|_{\mathrm{TV}}=\sup_A|P(A)-Q_o(A)|, Pinsker's inequality gives

\|P-Q_o\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\le\sqrt{\tfrac12 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(Q_o\|P)}.

The expected terminal loss difference is bounded by L_{\max}\|P-Q_o\|_{\mathrm{TV}}. Taking expectations and applying Jensen:

VOC_1\le L_{\max}\mathbb{E}_o\|P-Q_o\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
\le L_{\max}\sqrt{\tfrac12\mathbb{E}_o D_{\mathrm{KL}}(Q_o\|P)}
=L_{\max}\sqrt{\tfrac{IG}{2}}.
\qquad\blacksquare

Remark 9.1. Theorem 9.2 is stated for myopic VOC_1. Dynamic DV may exceed this bound because it accounts for future verification options; the bound does not extend automatically to VOC^\star or to dynamic DV without matching the loss object, the resource budget, and the future state definition.

Corollary 9.1 (Minimum information price — necessary condition for myopic worthwhileness). If verification is worthwhile in the myopic sense (VOC_1>c_V), then

IG(s)>\frac{2c_V^2}{L_{\max}^2}.

Remark 9.2. Corollary 9.1 is a necessary condition for myopic worthwhileness. A dynamic policy can be worthwhile even when VOC_1\le c_V, because verification may create option value or complementarities across candidates.

