Appendix B — Detailed Proofs

B.1 Lemma 3.1 — Acceptance Probability

Statement. P(O=\mathrm{acc})=(1-\epsilon)p_A.

Proof. By the law of total probability,

P(O=\mathrm{acc})=P(O=\mathrm{acc}\mid A=1)P(A=1)+P(O=\mathrm{acc}\mid A=0)P(A=0).

By soundness (SA1), P(O=\mathrm{acc}\mid A=0)=0. By definition, P(O=\mathrm{acc}\mid A=1)=1-\epsilon. Therefore

P(O=\mathrm{acc})=(1-\epsilon)p_A.
\qquad\blacksquare

B.2 Lemma 3.2 — Rejection Posterior

Statement. P(A=1\mid O=\mathrm{rej})=\dfrac{\epsilon p_A}{1-(1-\epsilon)p_A}.

Proof. By Bayes' theorem,

P(A=1\mid O=\mathrm{rej})=\frac{P(O=\mathrm{rej}\mid A=1)P(A=1)}{P(O=\mathrm{rej})}.

The numerator is \epsilon p_A. The denominator is 1-P(O=\mathrm{acc})=1-(1-\epsilon)p_A by Lemma 3.1. \blacksquare

B.3 Corollary 3.1 — Certification Asymmetry

Statement. If 0<p_A<1 and 0<\epsilon<1, then P(A=1\mid O=\mathrm{acc})=1 and 0<P(A=1\mid O=\mathrm{rej})<1.

Proof. For acceptance:

P(A=1\mid O=\mathrm{acc})=\frac{P(O=\mathrm{acc}\mid A=1)P(A=1)}{P(O=\mathrm{acc})}=\frac{(1-\epsilon)p_A}{(1-\epsilon)p_A}=1,

provided P(O=\mathrm{acc})>0, which holds when p_A>0 and \epsilon<1.

For rejection: the numerator \epsilon p_A>0 and the denominator 1-(1-\epsilon)p_A>0. The upper bound is strict because p_A<1 implies 1-(1-\epsilon)p_A>1-p_A>0 and \epsilon p_A<1-(1-\epsilon)p_A. \blacksquare

B.4 n=1 Sanity Check (Detailed)

Setup. n=1, candidate has validity probability p_1, verifier error \epsilon_1, cost c_1, terminal penalty P_{\mathrm{bad}}.

Without verification. Submit candidate 1. Expected loss:

\mathbb{E}[L]=P_{\mathrm{bad}}\cdot P(A_1=0)=P_{\mathrm{bad}}(1-p_1).

With verification.

· Accept (probability \alpha_1=p_1(1-\epsilon_1)): loss 0.
· Reject (probability 1-\alpha_1): submit candidate 1, expected loss P_{\mathrm{bad}}\cdot P(A_1=0\mid O_1=\mathrm{rej}).

Rejection posterior:

P(A_1=1\mid O_1=\mathrm{rej})=\frac{\epsilon_1 p_1}{1-\alpha_1},\qquad
P(A_1=0\mid O_1=\mathrm{rej})=1-\frac{\epsilon_1 p_1}{1-\alpha_1}=\frac{1-\alpha_1-\epsilon_1 p_1}{1-\alpha_1}.

So expected terminal loss after rejection is

(1-\alpha_1)\cdot P_{\mathrm{bad}}\cdot\frac{1-\alpha_1-\epsilon_1 p_1}{1-\alpha_1}=P_{\mathrm{bad}}(1-\alpha_1-\epsilon_1 p_1).

Now

1-\alpha_1-\epsilon_1 p_1=1-p_1(1-\epsilon_1)-\epsilon_1 p_1=1-p_1.

Therefore the expected terminal loss is P_{\mathrm{bad}}(1-p_1), identical to the no-verification case. Gross DV is zero. Net DV is -c_1. \blacksquare

B.5 Theorem 4.1 — Exact Zero-Value Characterization

Statement. VOC_1(s)=0 iff there exists a^\star\in\arg\min_a\mathbb{E}[C(a,\Theta)\mid s] such that a^\star\in\arg\min_a\mathbb{E}[C(a,\Theta)\mid s,o] for P-a.e. o.

Proof. Let a^\star\in\arg\min_a\mathbb{E}[C(a,\Theta)\mid s]. By the law of total expectation,

R(s)=\mathbb{E}[C(a^\star,\Theta)\mid s]=\mathbb{E}_o[\mathbb{E}[C(a^\star,\Theta)\mid s,o]].

Therefore

VOC_1(s)=R(s)-\mathbb{E}_o[R(s,o)]=\mathbb{E}_o[\mathbb{E}[C(a^\star,\Theta)\mid s,o]-R(s,o)].

The integrand is nonnegative because R(s,o) is the minimum over a. Hence VOC_1(s)\ge0, with equality iff the integrand vanishes P-a.e., i.e., a^\star\in\arg\min_a\mathbb{E}[C(a,\Theta)\mid s,o] for P-a.e. o. \blacksquare

B.6 Theorem 4.2 — Gap Decomposition

Statement. For a^\star\in\arg\min_a\mathbb{E}[C(a,\Theta)\mid s] and a^\star(o)\in\arg\min_a\mathbb{E}[C(a,\Theta)\mid s,o], define

\mathrm{gap}(o)=\mathbb{E}[C(a^\star,\Theta)\mid s,o]-\mathbb{E}[C(a^\star(o),\Theta)\mid s,o]\ge0.

Then VOC_1(s)=\mathbb{E}_O[\mathrm{gap}(O)].

Proof. Immediate from the law of total expectation:

VOC_1(s)=\mathbb{E}_o\left[\mathbb{E}[C(a^\star,\Theta)\mid s,o]-R(s,o)\right]=\mathbb{E}_o[\mathrm{gap}(o)].
\qquad\blacksquare

Lower bound. Let p_{\mathrm{change}}=P(a^\star\notin\arg\min_a\mathbb{E}[C(a,\Theta)\mid s,o]). On the change event, \mathrm{gap}(o)\ge g_{\min} by definition. On the complement, \mathrm{gap}(o)\ge0. Therefore

VOC_1(s)\ge p_{\mathrm{change}}\cdot g_{\min}.

For continuous observation spaces, replace g_{\min} by \operatorname*{ess\,inf}. \blacksquare

B.7 Counterexample to Simple Additivity

Setup. s=A\wedge B. Each subgoal costs 1, succeeds with probability 1/2, failure incurs penalty P=10.

Individual costs.

C^\star(A)=1+\tfrac12\cdot 10=6,\qquad C^\star(B)=1+\tfrac12\cdot 10=6.

Sum: 12.

Conjunction. Try A; if succeeds, try B:

C^\star(A\wedge B)=1+\tfrac12\cdot 1+\left(\tfrac12+\tfrac14\right)\cdot 10=1+0.5+7.5=9.

Therefore C^\star(A\wedge B)=9\ne12. The Bellman decomposition fails because the terminal loss is not additive across AND-components. \blacksquare

B.8 Theorem 5.1 — Additivity Under Additive Terminal Loss

Statement. Under Definition 5.1 plus additive terminal loss L_{\mathrm{terminal}}(s_1\wedge\cdots\wedge s_r)=\sum_k L_k(s_k), we have C^\star(s_1\wedge\cdots\wedge s_r)=\sum_k C^\star(s_k).

Proof. The Bellman operator decomposes:

C^\star(s_1\wedge\cdots\wedge s_r)=\min_{a_1,\dots,a_r}\sum_k\mathbb{E}[C_k(s_k,a_k)]=\sum_k\min_{a_k}\mathbb{E}[C_k(s_k,a_k)]=\sum_k C^\star(s_k).

The key step is that the min and sum commute under additive costs and additive terminal loss. \blacksquare

B.9 Theorem 5.2 — Sign of \kappa_{\mathrm{VOC}}

Positive example (XOR). Let X,Y\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(1/2) independent, a^\star=X\oplus Y, cost 0 for correct and 1 otherwise.

· No observation: R=1/2.
· o_1=X alone: Y remains 50/50, so R=1/2; VOC_1(o_1)=0.
· o_2=Y alone: similarly VOC_1(o_2)=0.
· Both: a^\star determined, R=0; VOC_1(o_1,o_2)=1/2.

Thus \kappa_{\mathrm{VOC}}=1/2-0-0=1/2>0.

Negative example (redundant observations). Let X\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(1/2), a^\star=X, cost 0 for correct and 1 otherwise.

· No observation: R=1/2.
· o_1=X alone: R=0, VOC_1(o_1)=1/2.
· o_1=o_2=X: R=0, VOC_1(o_1,o_2)=1/2.

Thus \kappa_{\mathrm{VOC}}=1/2-1/2-1/2=-1/2<0.

Zero example. Independent observations on disjoint components of an additive cost yield \kappa_{\mathrm{VOC}}=0 by Theorem 5.1. \blacksquare

B.10 Theorem 6.1 — Optimal Ordering for Fixed S

Statement. Fix S\subseteq\{1,\dots,n\}. The order minimizing C_{\mathrm{full}}(\cdot,S) is by decreasing \alpha_i/c_i.

Proof (adjacent interchange). Fix positions k,k+1 with candidates i,j, and let \beta=\prod_{m<k}(1-\alpha_{\sigma(m)}). The contribution of the two positions is

\beta[c_i+(1-\alpha_i)c_j]\quad\text{for }(i,j),

\beta[c_j+(1-\alpha_j)c_i]\quad\text{for }(j,i).

The terminal term is identical because (1-\alpha_i)(1-\alpha_j)=(1-\alpha_j)(1-\alpha_i). Therefore (i,j) is at least as good as (j,i) iff

c_i+(1-\alpha_i)c_j\le c_j+(1-\alpha_j)c_i\iff \alpha_j c_i\le\alpha_i c_j\iff \frac{\alpha_i}{c_i}\ge\frac{\alpha_j}{c_j}.

Any order with an adjacent inversion is strictly improvable by swapping. \blacksquare

B.11 Theorem 9.2 — Pinsker Upper Bound

Statement. Under bounded loss L_{\max}, Z-dependence, and IG=I(Z;O\mid s) in nats:

VOC_1(s)\le L_{\max}\sqrt{\frac{IG(s)}{2}}.

Proof. Let P be the prior on Z and Q_o the posterior given O=o. With sup-norm TV,

\|P-Q_o\|_{\mathrm{TV}}=\sup_A|P(A)-Q_o(A)|.

Pinsker's inequality:

\|P-Q_o\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\le\sqrt{\tfrac12 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(Q_o\|P)}.

The expected terminal loss difference is bounded by L_{\max}\|P-Q_o\|_{\mathrm{TV}}. Taking expectations:

VOC_1(s)\le L_{\max}\mathbb{E}_o\|P-Q_o\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

By Jensen's inequality and the concavity of \sqrt{\cdot}:

\mathbb{E}_o\sqrt{\tfrac12 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(Q_o\|P)}\le\sqrt{\tfrac12\mathbb{E}_o D_{\mathrm{KL}}(Q_o\|P)}.

The standard identity IG=\mathbb{E}_o D_{\mathrm{KL}}(Q_o\|P) gives the result. \blacksquare

