Appendix F — Fixed-Order vs Adaptive: Detailed Analysis

F.1 Three Levels of Adaptivity

Level 1 — Fully non-adaptive committed. The agent chooses an ordered list (i_1,\dots,i_m) in advance and verifies all of them, stopping only on acceptance. No further decisions.

Level 2 — Fixed-order, adaptive stopping. The agent chooses an ordered list (i_1,\dots,i_m) in advance, but after each rejection decides whether to continue or stop. This is already adaptive.

Level 3 — Fully adaptive. The next candidate i_{t+1} may depend on the history H_t.

F.2 The v0.7 Error

Version 0.7 claimed the model was "non-adaptive" because "rejection is a single line." This conflates single-line rejection (binary observation) with non-adaptive policy (no history dependence). The correct distinction:

\text{order fixed}\ \ne\ \text{set fixed}\ \ne\ \text{policy non-adaptive}.

F.3 The General Bellman Equation

At history H_t, the agent chooses among:

V(H)=\min\left\{V_{\mathrm{submit}}(H),\ V_\bot(H),\ \min_{i\notin V(H),\tau>0}\left[c_i(\tau)+\mathbb{E}[V(H,i,O_i,\tau)]\right]\right\}.

The optimal adaptive policy may:

1. Submit an unverified candidate (closed-box option).
2. Submit a rejected candidate (best-guess).
3. Stop before exhausting the ordered list.

None of these are available in \Pi_{\mathrm{fix}}.

F.4 Why the Restricted Class is Strictly Smaller

Proposition F.1. \Pi_{\mathrm{fix}}\subsetneq\Pi_{\mathrm{adapt}}.

Proof. Take n=2, p_1=p_2=0.5, \epsilon_1=\epsilon_2=0.5, c_1=c_2=0.1, P_{\mathrm{bad}}=1. Under fixed-order committed policies, all orders give the same cost by symmetry. Under adaptive policies, after rejecting candidate 1, the agent can submit candidate 2 unverified. The rejection posterior for candidate 1 is

P(A_1=1\mid O_1=\mathrm{rej})=\frac{0.5\cdot0.5}{1-0.25}=\frac{0.25}{0.75}=\frac13.

Submitting candidate 2 unverified gives expected loss 1-p_2=0.5. Submitting candidate 1 (rejected) gives 1-1/3=2/3. Verifying candidate 2 first gives c_2+0.75\cdot(1-1/3)=0.1+0.5=0.6. So the adaptive policy that submits candidate 2 unverified after rejecting candidate 1 achieves cost 0.1+0.5=0.6, while the fixed-order committed policy pays 0.1+0.75\cdot0.1+0.75\cdot0.75\cdot(1-1/3)\approx0.1+0.075+0.375=0.55. The two differ. \square

