Appendix G — Resource-Dependent Verification: Detailed Derivation

G.1 The Two-Sample Model

Assumption G.1. The resource requirement T_i of candidate i has CDF F_i^{(1)} given A_i=1 and F_i^{(0)} given A_i=0.

Assumption G.2. Verification accepts iff T_i\le\tau and A_i=1.

Consequence G.1. \alpha_i(\tau)=p_i F_i^{(1)}(\tau).

Consequence G.2.

\epsilon_i(\tau)=1-F_i^{(1)}(\tau).

G.2 Expected Cost Derivation

The expected verification cost is

c_i(\tau)=\lambda_V\mathbb{E}[\min(T_i,\tau)].

By the law of total expectation,

\mathbb{E}[\min(T_i,\tau)]=p_i\mathbb{E}[\min(T_i^{(1)},\tau)]+(1-p_i)\mathbb{E}[\min(T_i^{(0)},\tau)].

Therefore

\boxed{c_i(\tau)=\lambda_V\left[p_i\int_0^\tau(1-F_i^{(1)}(t))\,dt+(1-p_i)\int_0^\tau(1-F_i^{(0)}(t))\,dt\right].}

G.3 Escalation Analysis

After rejection at \tau_1, the conditional success probability given validity at budget \tau_2>\tau_1:

P(T_i^{(1)}\le\tau_2\mid T_i^{(1)}>\tau_1)=\frac{F_i^{(1)}(\tau_2)-F_i^{(1)}(\tau_1)}{1-F_i^{(1)}(\tau_1)}.

This replaces the independent-redraw assumption of classical search theory.

G.4 The Resource-Dependent Ratio

\rho_i(\tau)=\frac{p_i F_i^{(1)}(\tau)}{\lambda_V\left[p_i\int_0^\tau(1-F_i^{(1)})+(1-p_i)\int_0^\tau(1-F_i^{(0)})\right]}.

G.5 Structural Difference from Classical Search Theory

In classical Koopman–Stone search, \epsilon is a fixed detector property. In the Lean model, \epsilon_i(\tau) is a design variable. This changes the action space from \{1,\dots,n\} to \{1,\dots,n\}\times(0,\infty) and introduces a joint candidate-budget optimization problem.

