Appendix K — The Central Result, Restated

The framework's main theorem, restricted to the fixed-order committed class, is:

\boxed{
C_{\mathrm{full}}(\sigma,S)\ \text{is minimized by ordering }S\text{ by decreasing }\alpha_i/c_i=p_i(1-\epsilon_i)/c_i.
}

The framework's main bounds, for myopic VOC_1, are:

\boxed{
p_{\mathrm{change}}\cdot g_{\min}\le VOC_1(s)\le L_{\max}\sqrt{\frac{IG(s)}{2}}.
}

The framework's main necessary condition for myopic worthwhileness is:

\boxed{
VOC_1(s)>c_V\implies IG(s)>\frac{2c_V^2}{L_{\max}^2}.
}

The framework's main open problem is the adaptive Bellman equation of §7:

\boxed{
V(H)=\min\left\{V_{\mathrm{submit}}(H),\ V_\bot(H),\ \min_{i\notin V(H),\tau>0}\left[c_i(\tau)+\mathbb{E}[V(H,i,O_i,\tau)]\right]\right\}.
}

The framework's main modeling hypothesis for Lean is:

\boxed{
\epsilon_i(\tau)=1-F_i^{(1)}(\tau),\qquad
c_i(\tau)=\lambda_V\left[p_i\mathbb{E}[\min(T_i^{(1)},\tau)]+(1-p_i)\mathbb{E}[\min(T_i^{(0)},\tau)]\right].
}

The central question remains:

\boxed{\text{When does verified information produce a measurable reduction in future proof-search cost?}}

The answer within the restricted fixed-order committed class is complete. The answer within the general adaptive class is open, and constitutes the research frontier of this program.

